  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1229 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nPrecipitation & Storm Chances Increase Through The Weekend:\n\nDry conditions expected through the rest of the work week as \neventual low level moisture transport excites through the Central \nPlains overnight Friday into Saturday, poking through southwest into \ncentral Minnesota. Similar to previous forecasts, the GEFS long term \nglobal ensemble mean solution suggests a stronger lobe of positive \nmid level vorticity advection running the ridge axis, resulting in \nearlier aforementioned precipitation chances initially overnight \nwhile EPS solution delays onset until the late morning/early \nafternoon Saturday. Although, eventual and perpetual stronger mid \nlevel vorticity advection through the weekend in the EPS results in \nhigher probabilities (100%) for 0.1\" of rainfall in 24 hours as the \nGEFS remains <80% until east of the forecast area in eastern \nWisconsin on Sunday. \n\nStorm Probabilities Sunday & Monday:\n\nConcurrent probabilities between and within long term global \nensembles has increased for instability settling near or over the \nforecast area for Sunday, increasing local storm concerns. While \nrelatively higher spread seen in EPS MUCAPE, both the GEFS and EPS \nexhibit joint probabilities of 30-60% for MUCAPE/SBCAPE of 500+ J/kg \nwith 30+ kts of 0-500mb bulk wind shear over the forecast area. \nAlthough, associated soundings suggest a low level capping inversion \npotentially limiting access to increased instability off the surface \nuntil Monday when long, skinny profiles limit higher value tail and \nconsequently overall ensemble probabilities. All accompanying \nmachine learning convective hazard forecast models limit local \nprobabilities to 15% given ensemble spread and conditional pattern. \nFurther confidence challenge stems from domino effect of initial \nstorm coverage(s) affecting subsequent storm initiation location(s) \nand strength(s) through the weekend.",
